                id: "fillet-bulge",
                title: "Fillet merge bulges flat faces: fix it or accept it?",
                source: "docs/state.md retained, Geometry: smooth-min bulges flush faces (<= r/4) (4-2)",
                description: r#"`fillet-merge` (Merge with fillet) blends two shapes with a smooth minimum. Where the shapes meet at an angle, as in the T on the right, that is the rounded inside corner you asked for. Where their faces line up, as in the flush pair on the left, it also pushes a bump out of the flat face at the seam, up to a quarter of the radius (0.1 m here, radius 0.4 m).

Fixing it means an exact fillet that rounds only the corners where faces meet at an angle. That is a geometry brief of its own, about the size of 4-2. Accepting it keeps today's behaviour and moves the brief's effort elsewhere.

Use "Side by side" to see both at once. Zoom in on the left pair's top and bottom faces, and on the T's inside corners."#,
                side_by_side: true,
                options: [
                    (
                        label: "Accept the bulge",
                        caption: "Today's fillet everywhere: the flush pair bulges at its seam, the T's corners are rounded.",
                        scripts: ["scenes/fillet-bulge/parts.scm", "scenes/fillet-bulge/today.scm"],
                    ),
                    (
                        label: "Fix it (exact fillet)",
                        caption: "What the fix gives: the flush pair's faces stay flat (shown with a plain merge), the T's corners are still rounded.",
                        scripts: ["scenes/fillet-bulge/parts.scm", "scenes/fillet-bulge/fixed.scm"],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            answer: (
