            question: (
                id: "script-unit-verbs",
                title: "How does a script write a number in a unit?",
                source: "brief 5-4 Goal (a quantity literal or constructor that converts to SI); docs/units-decision.md P6",
                description: r#"Verbs take SI and must keep doing so. A number in another unit can be written with one verb per unit ((cm 25), (deg 90)) or with one verb that takes the unit as text ((q 25 "cm")). The unit table holds min, t, g, in, h and s, which a script already uses as names (min is a Scheme built-in)."#,
                decision: true,
                options: [
                    (
                        label: "One verb, the unit as text",
                        caption: r#"Recommended. (q 25 "cm"), plus (convert v from to) and (unit-of u). The unit text is anything a number field reads (km/h, N m, deg/s), through the same parser. Short names are one define each, shown in docs/scripting.md. No new global names that shadow a script's own."#,
                    ),
                    (
                        label: "One verb per unit",
                        caption: r#"(cm 25) reads shortest, but adds about thirty global names, some of them (min, t, g, in) already in use, and a composed unit such as km/h has no verb."#,
                    ),
                ],
            ),
